#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175701 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "00cf6180-070b-5ebf-af24-bc4c4b0bd94e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Complex Eigenvector of a Matrix Given a Complex Eigenvalue"}] :question/id #uuid "e65b269e-a33e-5bc6-b13a-c3f12488f260" :question/math-academy-id "e-5527" :question/problem "Find an eigenvector of\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 4 \\\\ -4 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=-3+4\\textrm{i}$." :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation\n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\,\\lambda =-3+4\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA - λI &= \\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 4 \\\\ -4 & -3\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}-3 + 4\\textrm{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 + 4\\textrm{i}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-4\\textrm{i} & 4 \\\\ -4 & -4\\textrm{i}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{-4\\textrm{i}x_{1} + 4x_{2} = 0; - 4x_{1} - 4\\textrm{i}x_{2} = 0\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(-3+4\\textrm{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another.\n\nAs a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\nx_1 = -\\textrm{i}x_2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\textrm{i}x_{2} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSetting $x_2=1$, we get the eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}-\\textrm{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175702 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0e83dcf-3ce6-5583-bd28-fdbc805a9403" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Complex Eigenvalue of a Matrix Given a Complex Eigenvector"}] :question/id #uuid "acaa8e8f-de36-56b0-8e02-ded7fd08118c" :question/math-academy-id "e-5528" :question/problem "Determine whether $\\begin{bmatrix}1 + 3\\textrm{i} \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -5 \\\\ 8 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" :question/worked-solution "The eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $\\mathbf{v}$ such that\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{v}=\\lambda \\mathbf{v}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAv &= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -5 \\\\ 8 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 + 3\\textrm{i} \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-18 + 6\\textrm{i} \\\\ 24\\textrm{i}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}18\\textrm{i}^{2} + 6\\textrm{i} \\\\ 24\\textrm{i}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= 6\\textrm{i}\\begin{bmatrix}1 + 3\\textrm{i} \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= 6\\textrm{i}v\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{v} = 6\\textrm{i} \\mathbf{v}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\lambda=6\\textrm{i}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175703 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ad17d11-325f-5888-a5cd-61ee27f7a103" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Two Linearly Independent Complex Eigenvectors of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "da3e8948-1f17-5281-a2f7-2fb1732939d9" :question/math-academy-id "e-5529" :question/problem "Let $\\begin{bmatrix}a_{1} \\\\ b_{1}\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}a_{2} \\\\ b_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$ be linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix\n$\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$\nGiven that $\\lambda_1=4+5\\textrm{i}$ is an eigenvalue of $A$, find the value of ${\\dfrac{a_1a_2}{b_1b_2}}$." :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation\n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\,\\lambda_1 =4+5\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA - λ_{1}I &= \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 4\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}4 + 5\\textrm{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 + 5\\textrm{i}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-5\\textrm{i} & 5 \\\\ -5 & -5\\textrm{i}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{-5\\textrm{i}x_{1} + 5x_{2} = 0; - 5x_{1} - 5\\textrm{i}x_{2} = 0\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(4+5\\textrm{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another.\n\nAs a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx_2 = \\textrm{i}x_1\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ \\textrm{i}x_{1}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSetting $x_1=1$, we get the eigenvector\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ \\textrm{i}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=4+5\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nis a complex eigenvalue of $A$, the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the complex conjugate of $\\lambda_1$. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda_2=\\overline{\\lambda_1}=4-5\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nMoreover, the corresponding eigenvector must be the complex conjugate of $\\mathbf v_1$. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -\\textrm{i}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\dfrac{a_1a_2}{b_1b_2}} = {\\dfrac{(1)(1)}{(\\textrm{i})(-\\textrm{i})}} = 1\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176393 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0e83dcf-3ce6-5583-bd28-fdbc805a9403" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Complex Eigenvalue of a Matrix Given a Complex Eigenvector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f1dd336-9ead-5c21-ac6f-768a4eb24855" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "λ = 3\\textrm{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174901} {:answer/id #uuid "961a2918-8746-55b0-9f8a-3e0bf9ef7e78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v$ is not an eigenvector of $A$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174902} {:answer/id #uuid "ba70b2fc-ceaa-5d8c-92ef-0cd4b5cd381f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "λ = 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174903} {:answer/id #uuid "c823c9ff-fca6-5959-89d1-86a2418f9e86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "λ =-3\\textrm{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174904} {:answer/id #uuid "6cacf0bc-517f-5104-82fa-779e5d09c203" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "λ = \\textrm{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174905}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f1dd336-9ead-5c21-ac6f-768a4eb24855" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "λ = 3\\textrm{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174901} :answer-field/id #uuid "733f9f3a-1888-5241-8b1b-05a81df288bd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174900}] :question/id #uuid "c6beb736-d02a-5b38-8238-42457ab554c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-38475" :question/problem "Determine whether $v = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 - 2\\textrm{i}\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of the matrix $A = \\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -3 \\\\ 6 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176396 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ad17d11-325f-5888-a5cd-61ee27f7a103" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Two Linearly Independent Complex Eigenvectors of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c18520c3-2049-51c7-8fe5-765c1abe198a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174919} {:answer/id #uuid "db908152-aff0-5533-ab91-4b47d766321f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174920} {:answer/id #uuid "13d3d5e2-31cf-5838-803e-e3a849c8ef8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174921} {:answer/id #uuid "1d33a334-e204-5efd-b71c-1deeeaf61aa5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174922} {:answer/id #uuid "45f41a32-8463-5143-8a14-060c4971deb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174923}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "13d3d5e2-31cf-5838-803e-e3a849c8ef8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174921} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9f3a2ce-5a9b-5da7-b976-ffe3fc139874" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174918}] :question/id #uuid "b212f7d8-65d4-5b14-984d-a9ab26791d27" :question/math-academy-id "q-38480" :question/problem "Given that $v_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}a_{1} \\\\ b_{1}\\end{bmatrix}$ and $v_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}a_{2} \\\\ b_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$ are linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix\n\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -4 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$,\nwhat is the value of $\\frac{a_{1}a_{2}}{b_{1}b_{2}}$?"}]}
